// Append-only history of user-account deactivations (issue #86). A row is
// written and never amended, so — like `AssetActivity` — the model carries no
// `updatedAt`; the absence is deliberate.
//
// This exists because `User.banReason` holds only the *current* state:
// reactivating clears it, and the reason then no longer exists anywhere. A
// deactivation writes both — `banReason` for what the admin users table shows
// now, a row here for what happened — and a reactivation clears `banReason`
// while writing the reason it cleared into a `reactivated` row, so the history
// outlives the state.
//
// A separate table rather than a widened `AssetActivity`: that model's `assetId`
// is `NOT NULL` with a cascade to `Asset`, so every row there belongs to a real
// asset. Making it nullable to fit rows that are not about assets would weaken
// that invariant for every existing row. This costs one `CREATE TABLE` and
// changes nothing that already exists.
//
// Both columns point at `user`, so each relation needs an explicit name —
// Prisma cannot pair two relations to the same model by field order. `userId`
// cascades, matching `session` and `account`: an account's own history goes with
// the account. `actorId` restricts, matching every other actor column in this
// schema, so an admin who has acted cannot be deleted out from under the trail.
//
// `reason` is restricted data. It is read by the admin users surface only, and
// no public or non-admin query selects this table at all.

model UserActivity {
  id        String           @id @default(uuid(7))
  userId    String
  user      User             @relation("UserActivitySubject", fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  actorId   String
  actor     User             @relation("UserActivityActor", fields: [actorId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  type      UserActivityType
  reason    String
  createdAt DateTime         @default(now())

  @@index([userId, createdAt])
  @@index([actorId])
  @@map("user_activity")
}
